{{smaller block|Compare my worth with others' base desert,<br>Let virtue be the ''touchstone'' of my love.
{{right|''Drayton'', Idea, lx.}}}}

¶
'''touchwood''' (tuch′wu̇d), ''n.'' [Appar. < ''touch'' + ''wood''<sup>1</sup>; cf. ''touch-paper''. According to Skeat, an altered form, simulating ''touch'', of ''tache-wood'', < ''tache''<sup>2</sup> + ''wood''<sup>1</sup>.] The soft white or yellowish substance into which wood is converted by the action of certain fungi: so called from its property of burning for many hours, when once ignited, like tinder. {{smaller|When the mycelium is in great abundance, it is sometimes observed to be luminous. The name ''touchwood'' is also applied to the fungus ''Polyporus igniarius.'' See ''spunk'', 1, ''amadou'', ''Polyporus''.}}

'''touchy''' (tuch′i), ''a.'' [A later form of ''tachy'', ''techy'', ''tetchy'', simulating ''touch'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>. See ''techy''. In def. 2 directly < ''touch'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. Apt to take offense on slight provocation; irritable; irascible; peevish; testy; tetchy.

{{smaller block|''Cal.'' If I durst fight, your tongue would lie at quiet.<br>''Mel.'' Y' are ''touchie'' without all cause.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid's Tragedy, iii.}}}}

{{smaller block|Take heed, my wit of the world! this is no age for wasps; 'tis a dangerous ''touchy'' age, and will not endure the stinging.
{{right|''Randolph'', Hey for Honesty, Int.}}}}

{{smaller block|You tell me that you apprehend<br>My verse may ''touchy'' folks offend.
{{right|''Gay'', Fables, iv.}}}}

2. In ''decorative art'', made up of small points, broken lines, or touches, and not drawn in a

readings.c1s2:
firm unbroken line, as the outline of any pattern. [Colloq.]

'''touffon†''', ''n.'' See ''typhoon.''

'''tough''' (tuf), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Formerly spelled also ''tuff;'' < ME. ''tough, towgh, tou, toȝ,'' < AS. ''tōh'' = MD. ''taey,'' D. ''taai'' = MLG. ''tā, taie, tege, teie,'' LG. ''taa, taë, taag, tage'' = OHG. ''zāhi,'' MHG. ''zæhe,'' G. ''zähe, zäh,'' G. dial. ''zach,'' tough. For the noun use, cf. equiv. ''rough''<sup>2</sup>, associated with ''rough''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.'', but prob. a sophisticated form of ''ruff'' for ''ruffian.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Having the property of flexibility without brittleness; yielding to a bending force without breaking; also, hard to cut or sever, as with a cutting-instrument: as, ''tough'' meat.

{{smaller block|Of bodies, some are fragile, and some are ''tough'' and not fragile.
{{right|''Bacon,'' Nat. Hist., § 841.}}}}

{{smaller block|Get me a cudgel, sirrah, and a ''tough'' one.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.,'' King and No King, v. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|And after this manner you may also keep gentles all winter, which is a good bait then, and much the better for being lively and ''tuffe.''
{{right|''I. Walton,'' Complete Angler (1653), xii.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|A goose of most promising figure, but which, at table, proved so inveterately ''tough'' that the carving-knife would make no impression on its carcass.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 20.}}}}

2. Firm; strong; able to endure hardship, hard work, or ill usage; hardy; not easily broken or impaired.

{{smaller block|The hauberkes of ''towgh'' mayle that the speres splyndred in peces.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 485.}}}}

{{smaller block|He 's well enough; he has a travell'd body,
And, though he be old, he 's ''tough'' and will endure well.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Pilgrim, v. 1.}}}}

3. Not easily separated; tenacious; stiff; ropy; viscous: as, a ''tough'' clay; ''tough'' phlegm.

{{smaller block|A cart that is overladen, going up a hill, draweth the horses back, and in a ''tough'' mire maketh them stand still.
{{right|''Tyndale'', Ans. to Sir T. More, etc. (Parker Soc., 1850), p. 211.}}}}

4. Not easily influenced; unyielding; stubborn; hardened; incorrigible.

{{smaller block|Callous and ''tough'',
The reprobated race grows judgment-proof.
{{right|Cowper, Table-Talk, l. 458.}}}}

{{smaller block|I found Mr. Macready . . . a ''tough'', sagacious, long-headed Scotchman.
{{right|''Scott'', Rob Roy, xiv.}}}}

5. Hard to manage or accomplish; difficult; trying; requiring great or continued effort. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|She [the town of Breda] has yielded up the Ghost to Spinola's Hands, after a ''tough'' Siege of thirteen Months, and a Circumvallation of near upon twenty Miles Compass.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. iv. 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|"My Lord," said the King, "here 's a rather ''tough'' job."
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, II. 69.}}}}

readings.c1s4:
¶
6. Severe; violent: as, a ''tough'' rebuke or tirade; a ''tough'' storm. [Colloq.]—{{smaller|'''Mild and tough,''' a phrase applied in some localities to fine brick-clay which has been mellowed or ripened by exposure. When fresh the clay is said to be ''short and rough.''—'''To make it tough†,''' to take pains; also, to make a difficulty about a thing; treat it as of great importance.—'''Tough pitch.''' See ''poling'', 2, and ''toughening''.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A rough; a bully; an incorrigibly vicious fellow; a bad character. [Colloq., U. S.]

{{smaller block|And then the whole appearance of the young ''tough'' changed, and the terror and horror that had showed on his face turned to one of low sharpness and evil cunning.
{{right|''Scribner's Mag.'', VIII. 692.}}}}

'''toughbark''' (tuf′bärk), ''n.'' See ''Pimelea''.

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The machine-tenders, of whom there are two to each Fourdrinier, work in ''tours'' or shifts twelve hours each.
{{right|''Harper's Mag.'', LXXV. 129.}}}}

3. A turn round some place; a going round from place to place; a continued ramble or excursion; a short journey: as, a wedding ''tour''.

{{smaller block|I must take a ''tour'' among the shops.
{{right|''Vanbrugh'', Confederacy, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Those who would make a curious journey, . . . might make a ''tour'' which I believe has not been done by any travellers, and that is to go along the eastern coast to Tarento.
{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, II. ii. 207, note.}}}}

{{smaller block|In a subsequent ''tour'' of observation, I encountered another of these relics of a "foregone world" locked up in the heart of the city.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 291.}}}}

{{smaller block|Bacon, however, made a ''tour'' through several provinces, and appears to have passed some time at Poitiers.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Lord Bacon.}}}}

4†. A turn, drive, or carriage promenade in a park or other place of fashionable resort for driving.

{{smaller block|The sweetness of the Park is at Eleven, when the Beau-Monde make their ''Tour'' there.
{{right|''Mrs. Centlivre'', The Basset Table, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Lucinda tells Sir Toby Doubtful: "You'll at least keep Six Horses, Sir Toby, for I wou'd not make a ''Tour'' in High Park with less for the World; for me thinks a pair looks like a Hackney."
{{right|''J. Ashton'', Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, II. 173.}}}}

5†. A fashionable drive, or resort for driving, as that in Hyde Park, London.

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Took up my wife and Deb., and to the Park, where, being in a hackney, and they undressed, was ashamed to go into the ''tour''.{{right|''Pepys'', Diary, March 31, 1668.}}}}

6†. Turn; cast; drift. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The whole ''tour'' of the passage is this: a man given to superstition can have no security, day or night, waking or sleeping.{{right|''Bentley'', Free-thinking, § 18.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Knight's tour.''' See ''knight.''—'''The grand tour''', a journey through France and Switzerland to Italy, etc., formerly considered essential for British young men of good family, as the finishing part of their education.=Syn. 3. ''Trip, Excursion'', etc. See ''journey''.}}

'''tour<sup>2</sup>''' (tör), ''v.'' [< ''tour''<sup>2</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' 1†. To turn.

{{smaller block|Each hundred you take here is as good as two or three hundred in New found Land; so that halfe the labour in hooking, splitting, and ''touring'' is saued.{{right|''Capt. John Smith'', Works, II. 188.}}}}

2. To make a tour; travel about.

{{smaller block|He was ''touring'' about as usual, for he was as restless as a hyena.{{right|''De Quincey'', Murder as One of the Fine Arts.}}}}

{{smaller block|It is like saying that a New Zealander ''touring'' in the British Isles sees that we are an aboriginal population.{{right|''A. Bain'', Emotions and Will, p. 517.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To make a tour or circuit of: as, to ''tour'' an island. [Rare.]

